WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Eigenvalues.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.171
Most vectors get knocked off course by a matrix.

3
00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.719
A few special ones don't.

4
00:00:06.719 --> 00:00:08.700
Let's find out why that matters.

5
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.500
When a matrix transforms a vector, the vector usually turns.

6
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.542
But along special directions, the matrix acts as pure scaling.

7
00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:19.533
Same direction, different length.

8
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.667
Here is a matrix, and three arrows.

9
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:26.100
Two of them sit on special directions, and one does not.

10
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.464
Apply the matrix.

11
00:00:27.464 --> 00:00:31.030
The green arrow stays on its line, stretched three times.

12
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.033
The blue one stays on its line, stretched twice.

13
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.151
The orange arrow gets knocked off its direction.

14
00:00:37.151 --> 00:00:38.267
It was not special.

15
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:40.767
Here is the equation.

16
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.230
A times v equals lambda times v.

17
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.800
The vector v is the special direction.

18
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.867
And lambda is the eigenvalue, the pure scale factor along it.

19
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:54.133
Along that direction, the whole matrix collapses into a single number.

20
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.433
In NumPy, one call finds them.

21
00:00:57.733 --> 00:01:02.367
We hand the matrix to eig, and it returns the eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

22
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:05.042
Then we check the equation for each pair.

23
00:01:05.042 --> 00:01:06.200
It holds every time.

24
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.500
And the scale factors are three and two.

25
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.667
The eigenvalues summarize what a matrix fundamentally does.

26
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:16.533
A big one means that direction is amplified.

27
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:18.567
A small one means it is dampened.

28
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.433
And a negative one flips it.

29
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:22.633
Now a real use.

30
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:26.700
A satellite streams sixty four correlated telemetry channels.

31
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.486
Compute the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix and you typically see three or four big ones, then a long tail near zero.

32
00:01:34.486 --> 00:01:40.500
The spacecraft state really lives in about four dimensions, so you know how much you can compress.

33
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.480
Let's recap.

34
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.533
Along special directions, a matrix just scales.

35
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:48.033
The eigenvalue is that scale factor.

36
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:52.300
Its size and sign say whether it amplifies, dampens, or flips.

37
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.142
And the whole spectrum shows how many dimensions matter.

38
00:01:56.142 --> 00:01:57.533
Next up: eigenvectors.
